  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 109 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nStrong upper level ridging will continue into next week allowing for \ndry conditions to persist. As high pressure settles over the region, \nwarm air advection will continue to push milder air into the region. \nTemperatures will remain above normals in the low to mid 60s, \npotentially even into the mid to upper 60s through much of next \nweek. The exception will Wednesday and Thursday as weak troughing \ndigs south just to the east of Nebraska. This will allow for some \ncooler air behind the trough dropping highs on both days across \nnorth central Nebraska potentially into the mid to upper 40s. \nElsewhere, highs will also drop, but only into the upper 50s to low \n60s. While temperatures will gradually warm back into the mid 60s by \nthe end of the week and into the weekend, highs across the north \ncentral area will be slow to increase, only rising into the mid 50s \nby Saturday. Overnight lows will also remain fairly mild in the mid \nto upper 30s through the week and even rising into the low 40s on \nTuesday night. However, the CAA pushing into the region mid-week \nwill also drop lows across the region. North central Nebraska will \nsee lows fall into the upper 20s with lows elsewhere dropping into \nthe low to mid 30s on both Wednesday and Thursday nights. In \naddition to cooler temperatures, some stronger wind gusts will be \npossible with this trough. There's a potential for gusts up to 35 \nmph across the Sandhills and north central Nebraska Wednesday \nafternoon as the trough pivots across the region. These stronger \nwinds will be brief, diminishing by Wednesday evening.",
